  "query_preview": ".. Per current MesoAnalysis, a closed mid-level low \nassociated with a large mid-level trough is slowly meandering east \nacross the Great Basin. With time, these features will deepen and \ngradually take on a neutral tilt before ejecting east of the area by \nWednesday afternoon. As deepening occurs, widespread ascent will \ncontinue atop widespread moisture renewing low elevation rainfall \nand high elevation snowfall chances across much of the forecast area \ntoday and Wednesday. Thankfully, with more modest and widespread \nascent, the threat for flash flooding is much lower today than prior \ndays. However, localized areal flooding remains possible given very \nsaturated soils. \n\nOn the backside of this low, cold temperatures will begin to fill in \nvia northerly flow. This will set the stage for areas such as \neastern Box Elder county, Juab / Millard counties, Sanpete Valley, \nand higher elevation valley locations across southwest Utah to see \nfreezing temperatures develop overnight tonight into Wednesday \nmorning. Additionally, there is a chance freezing temperatures \ndevelop across the Sevier Valley and portions of Duchesne county, \nthough probabilities for this were lower (<40%), with the higher \nprobabilities remaining confined to higher elevation locations. \nChances for freezing temperatures look to increase as we head into \nThursday and Friday mornings, with more freeze product issuances \nexpected over the coming days. \n\nA couple locations stand out regarding localized gusty winds over \nthe next few days, starting with gap winds across Washington county \novernight tonight and Wednesday evening into Thursday morning. As \nthe mid-level low progresses east, a tight northerly pressure \ngradient sets up across southwestern UT which may result in gusts to \naround 40mph along areas of I-15 in Washington county, potentially \nexceeding 40mph at times. However, confidence is lower regarding the \nduration and extremes of this event, thus precluding any wind \nproduct issuances at this time. Chances for higher gusts increase \nsomewhat into Thursday morning, and this will be ironed out over the \nnext few forecast cycles.\n\nThe next wind event in the forecast looks to occur as another large \nmid-level trough dives south across the Great Basin into the \nSouthwest, clocking mid-level winds to easterly. This looks to occur \naround the Thursday night / Friday morning timeframe, which would \nproduce gusty downslope winds across some of our western valleys \nalong N/S facing terrain. Given more modest flow aloft, this may end \nup as a canyon wind event favoring the Ogden-Farmington corridor, \nthough a few pieces of guidance carry gusts to around 30mph (some \nlocally higher) into the SLC corridor. No product issuance is \nplanned at this time, though will be worth monitoring over the next \nfew days. \n\nFollowing the departure of this system, zonal westerly flow briefly \nsets up across the Great Basin ahead of another Pacific system \nbringing generally quiet conditions through the weekend. As we enter \nnext week, ensemble guidance generally favors another trough \nimpacting the west with >80% of ensemble members supporting this \nsolution at this time. Currently, this looks to bring more \nprecipitation to much of the forecast area, as well as more mountain \nsnow given 700mb temperatures ranging from -2C to -5C. Guidance \nvaries regarding the southern extent of the system, thus influencing \nhow much cold air is drawn south, resulting in a notable spread \nregarding any snowfall accumulation at this time.",
